---
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const manifest = `{
  "endpoints": [
    {
      "id": "user-profile",
      "label": "User profile",
      "method": "GET",
      "path": "/api/users/:id",
      "scenarios": [
        {
          "id": "success",
          "label": "Successful response",
          "status": 200,
          "delay": 250,
          "file": "scenarios/user-success.json"
        },
        {
          "id": "not-found",
          "label": "404 Not Found",
          "status": 404,
          "file": "scenarios/user-not-found.json"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}`;

const rootDelay = `{
  "delay": 400
}`;

const directEndpoint = `{
  "endpoints": [
    {
      "id": "json-response",
      "label": "Simple JSON response",
      "method": "GET",
      "path": "/api/demo/json",
      "file": "scenarios/explicit.json",
      "status": 200,
      "delay": 150
    }
  ]
}`;

const directFile = `{
  "endpoints": [
    {
      "path": "/api/demo/json",
      "file": "scenarios/explicit.json"
    }
  ]
}`;

const noContent = `{
  "endpoints": [
    {
      "path": "/api/demo/json",
      "status": 204
    }
  ]
}`;
---

<DocsLayout title="Configuration" description="Plugin options, manifests, and file resolution." activePath="/configuration/">
    <article>
        <p class="kicker">Convention before configuration</p>
        <h1>File naming first. Manifest when needed.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
            Most projects only need local files. The plugin derives candidates from the request URL and HTTP method;
            configuration becomes useful when individual calls need exceptional behavior.
        </p>

        <h2 id="file-naming-conventions">File naming conventions</h2>
        <p>
            A URL becomes a path below <code>mockRoot</code>. For each candidate, the plugin checks the uppercased HTTP
            method prefix first and the generic filename second.
        </p>
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Request</th><th>Preferred file</th><th>Generic fallback</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>GET /api/users/profile</code></td><td><code>users/GET_profile.json</code></td><td><code>users/profile.json</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>POST /api/users/profile</code></td><td><code>users/POST_profile.json</code></td><td><code>users/profile.json</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>GET /api/reports/monthly</code></td><td><code>reports/GET_monthly.pdf</code></td><td><code>reports/monthly.pdf</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
            More specific route candidates are tried before suffix and parent fallbacks. First, all candidates for
            <code>users/profile</code> are checked; within each configured extension, the method-prefixed filename wins
            over the generic filename. Only then does resolution continue with the root-level <code>profile</code> suffix.
            Looking only at JSON, <code>PUT /api/users/profile</code> therefore checks
            <code>users/PUT_profile.json</code>, <code>users/profile.json</code>, <code>PUT_profile.json</code>, and
            <code>profile.json</code> in that order. Parent fallbacks follow afterwards.
        </p>
        <p>
            The <code>*</code> candidates use the configured extension order. Supported defaults include JSON, PDF, CSV,
            text, JPEG, PNG, and WebP.
        </p>

        <h2 id="plugin-options">Plugin options</h2>
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>mockRoot</code></td><td><code>URL</code></td><td>Directory containing mock files and the manifest.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>internalPrefix</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td>Request prefix handled by the plugin. Defaults to <code>/api/</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>extensions</code></td><td><code>string[]</code></td><td>Additional file extensions appended to the built-in candidates.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>contentTypes</code></td><td><code>Record&lt;string, string&gt;</code></td><td>Additional or overridden extension-to-content-type mappings.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>manifestFileName</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td>Manifest filename inside <code>mockRoot</code>. Defaults to <code>mock.manifest.json</code>.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <Callout title="Fixed route">
            <p><code>MANIFEST_ROUTE</code> is intentionally fixed and cannot be overridden by plugin options.</p>
        </Callout>

        <h2 id="optional-manifest">Optional manifest</h2>
        <p>
            Create <code>mock.manifest.json</code> only for endpoints that need additional handling. Other requests continue
            to use normal file resolution, so adopting a manifest is not an all-or-nothing decision.
        </p>

        <h3 id="root-fields">Root fields</h3>
        <p>
            The optional root-level <code>delay</code> applies to every mocked API request. This is already a complete
            manifest; an <code>endpoints</code> array is not required:
        </p>
        <Code code={rootDelay} label="mock/mock.manifest.json" />

        <h3 id="direct-endpoint-handling">Direct endpoint handling</h3>
        <p>
            An endpoint can define <code>file</code>, <code>status</code>, and <code>delay</code> directly, independently or
            in combination. Only <code>path</code> is required. Without <code>method</code>, the endpoint matches every HTTP
            method; without <code>file</code>, normal naming-convention resolution remains active.
        </p>
        <Code code={directEndpoint} label="complete endpoint configuration" />
        <Code code={directFile} label="minimal explicit response file" />
        <Code code={noContent} label="file-free 204 response" />
        <p>
            A direct <code>status: 204</code> is the exception to file resolution: it immediately returns a real empty
            response because a 204 response cannot have a body.
        </p>

        <h3 id="multiple-scenarios">Multiple scenarios</h3>
        <Code code={manifest} label="mock/mock.manifest.json" />
        <p>
            Exactly one configured scenario is selected automatically—no panel and no scenario cookie are needed. With
            multiple scenarios, the panel or scenario cookie chooses one. Until a choice exists, endpoint-level settings
            and then normal file resolution are used.
        </p>

        <h3 id="endpoint-fields">Endpoint fields</h3>
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Required</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>id</code></td><td>No</td><td>Stable ID for panel controls and cookies. When omitted, it is generated from method and path.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>method</code></td><td>No</td><td>HTTP method matched case-insensitively. When omitted, every method matches.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>path</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Exact path shape. Segments beginning with <code>:</code> match dynamic values.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>label</code></td><td>No</td><td>Human-readable name displayed in the proxy panel.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>active</code></td><td>No</td><td>Set to <code>false</code> to exclude the endpoint from scenario matching.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>file</code></td><td>No</td><td>Explicit response file relative to <code>mockRoot</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>status</code></td><td>No</td><td>HTTP response status. A file-free <code>204</code> returns an empty response.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>delay</code></td><td>No</td><td>Endpoint-specific delay in milliseconds.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>scenarios</code></td><td>No</td><td>One automatic or multiple selectable response variants.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3 id="scenario-fields">Scenario fields</h3>
        <p>
            Scenario <code>id</code> and <code>label</code> are optional. A missing ID is generated from the parent endpoint
            path and the scenario's one-based position; a missing label falls back to the explicit or generated ID. The
            optional <code>file</code> is relative to <code>mockRoot</code>; <code>status</code> changes the HTTP status and
            <code>delay</code> waits in milliseconds. If <code>file</code> is omitted, normal candidate resolution is used.
            Because generated scenario IDs include their position, define explicit IDs when selections must remain stable
            while scenarios are reordered. If a cookie references a scenario that no longer exists, the first configured
            scenario is used as a safe fallback.
        </p>
        <Callout title="Response precedence">
            <p>
                A selected or automatic scenario overrides endpoint-level <code>file</code>, <code>status</code>, and
                <code>delay</code>. Endpoint values override the root delay. Any remaining file lookup follows the naming
                conventions.
            </p>
        </Callout>
        <Callout title="Typical manifest use cases">
            <p>
                Return a real <code>204</code> response, simulate <code>400</code> or <code>500</code> failures, delay a call
                to test loading and timeout behavior, or point one endpoint at an explicit response file.
            </p>
        </Callout>
    </article>
</DocsLayout>
